  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "thm:shrinking-overlap-frontier",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "Fix \\(t_0>0\\), \\(\\zeta>0\\), and \\(0<M_1<\\sqrt{M_2}\\).  Let \\(\\delta_T\\ge0\\) be deterministic with \\(\\delta_T\\to0\\), put \\(C_T:=1+\\delta_T\\), \\(q_T:=q_{C_T}=\\delta_T/(1+\\delta_T)\\), and \\(\\beta:=2/(2+t_0\\zeta)\\).  There are constants \\(0<c_{\\mathrm{loc}}\\le C_{\\mathrm{loc}}<\\infty\\), depending only on \\(t_0,\\zeta,M_1,M_2\\), such that, for all sufficiently large \\(T\\),\n\\[\n c_{\\mathrm{loc}}\\{T^{-1}+T^{-\\beta}q_T^{2(1-\\beta)}\\}\n \\le R_T(t_0,\\zeta,C_T)\n \\le C_{\\mathrm{loc}}\\{T^{-1}+T^{-\\beta}q_T^{2(1-\\beta)}\\}.\n\\]\nThe observable estimator \\(\\widehat\\theta_{\\kappa_T(C_T)}\\) attains the upper bound.  The exact elbow is \\(q_T\\asymp T^{-1/2}\\), equivalently \\(C_T-1\\asymp T^{-1/2}\\) near equality: immediate weighting is parametric when \\(Tq_T^2=O(1)\\), whereas when \\(Tq_T^2\\to\\infty\\) the selected history is of order \\(\\log(Tq_T^2)\\) and the second risk term dominates.  There is no logarithmic factor in the risk.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(T\\)",
      "\\(t_0\\)",
      "\\(\\zeta\\)",
      "\\(M_1\\)",
      "\\(M_2\\)",
      "\\(\\delta_T\\)",
      "\\(C\\)",
      "\\(q_C\\)",
      "\\(\\beta\\)",
      "\\(R_T(t_0,\\zeta,C)\\)",
      "\\(\\widehat\\theta_k\\)",
      "\\(\\kappa_T\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:model-class",
      "def:minimax-risk",
      "def:phiw-estimator",
      "def:history-depth",
      "def:overlap-distance-radius",
      "def:local-frontier-handle",
      "thm:uniform-overlap-frontier"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "The specialization \\(C_T=1+\\delta_T\\), its normalized radius, and its adaptive depth are the objects recorded by \\(\\mathrm{def{:}local\\text{-}frontier\\text{-}handle}\\).  Apply \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}uniform\\text{-}overlap\\text{-}frontier}\\) with \\(C=C_T\\).  The definition \\(\\mathrm{def{:}overlap\\text{-}distance\\text{-}radius}\\) gives exactly\n\\[\n q_{C_T}=\\frac{C_T-1}{C_T}=\\frac{\\delta_T}{1+\\delta_T}.\n\\]\nThe uniform theorem supplies the two risk inequalities with constants depending only on \\(t_0,\\zeta,M_1,M_2\\), and \\(\\mathrm{def{:}history\\text{-}depth}\\) supplies the displayed observable estimator.  The ratio of the second risk term to the first is\n\\[\n \\frac{T^{-\\beta}q_T^{2(1-\\beta)}}{T^{-1}}\n =(Tq_T^2)^{1-\\beta}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(0<\\beta<1\\), this ratio is bounded when \\(Tq_T^2=O(1)\\) and diverges when \\(Tq_T^2\\to\\infty\\).  Moreover \\(\\delta_T\\to0\\) implies \\(q_T/\\delta_T=(1+\\delta_T)^{-1}\\to1\\), so \\(q_T\\asymp T^{-1/2}\\) is equivalent to \\(C_T-1=\\delta_T\\asymp T^{-1/2}\\).  On the nonparametric side of the elbow, the definition of \\(\\kappa_T(C_T)\\), together with the fact that its \\(T/2\\) cap is eventually inactive, gives \\(\\kappa_T(C_T)=\\lfloor\\log(Tq_T^2)/\\{2\\log(1/\\alpha)+\\log L\\}\\rfloor\\), which is of the asserted logarithmic order.  All conclusions therefore follow from the uniform theorem without any additional condition.",
    "justification": "This corollary gives the exact local elbow and removes the former open-transition language.",
    "gap": "The shrinking-radius regime was not characterized by the anchor result.",
    "consumer": "It converts a sequence of strengthening occupancy assurances into a concrete history rule.",
